{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall put on his linen tunic and linen breeches shall he put upon his flesh, and he shall take up the ashes.</b> He informs us here that even terumat ha-deshen (removal of the ashes), which is a light avodah (service), requires the priestly garments. For מדו בד (his linen garment) is the tunic (כתונת), and he uses the expression מדו — that it should be according to his measure; על בשרו (upon his flesh) — that there be nothing interposing between the tunic and his flesh, nor between the breeches and his flesh; and even if his breeches were short and did not reach down to his feet and he performed his service in them, his service is invalid. And the fact that he mentioned only these two — tunic and breeches — the same ruling applies to the rest of the garments: for once he has informed us that terumat ha-deshen requires garments, we learn with certainty that it requires four garments for an ordinary priest and eight for the High Priest. And so it states in the Sifrei: ילבש — to include the mitre (מצנפת) and the sash (אבנט)." "<b>And one may</b> further interpret מדו as 'his garments,' in the manner of (1 Samuel 17:39) ״מעל למדיו״, and similarly (Psalms 133:2) ״על פי מדותיו״; if so, it includes all the priestly garments, and this is what Onkelos translated: לבושין (garments). From this we learn that even in the matter of terumat ha-deshen, which is a light avodah, the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded that the priest adorn himself before Him — that he don the priestly garments and in them take up the ashes. All of this is an admonition and a way of life for everyone who draws near to the Holy One, blessed be He, in any of the avadot (forms of service), or whoever engages in any of the commandments of the Torah, that he should adorn himself before Him, blessed be He, in performing any one of the commandments of HaShem — that he should humble his own honor for the honor of the Supreme Essence, even in the lightest of them. And so David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 57:9): ״עורה כבודי עורה הנבל וכנור אעירה שחר״ — עורה כבודי: your honor counts as nothing beside the honor of your Maker; and it is written (2 Samuel 6:14): ״ודוד מכרכר בכל עז לפני ה׳״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ונקלותי עוד מזאת והייתי שפל בעיני״ and so forth." "<b>And know</b> from the way of the science of logic that a person is not called a servant (eved) unless he has a master (adon), and the name 'master' is fitting only for one who has a servant; and so these are two names, neither of which precedes the other. And since man is a servant to the Holy One, blessed be He, and is obligated to serve Him, the service cannot be complete until the servant takes upon himself all the traits of servitude — namely, submission and lowliness before Him. He must not conduct himself before Him with the trait of mastery and dominion, for that would be demeaning the honor of his Master. And Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 25:6): ״אל תתהדר לפני מלך״ — all the more so before the King of the kings of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He — because pride is His garment, as it is written (Psalms 93:1): ״ה׳ מלך גאות לבש״, and one who dons the garment of the King thereby demeans the King. For this reason the priest engaged in the avodah, uniquely designated for the service of the Temple, was commanded by Scripture to take up the ashes every day — which is the lightest of the avadot — in order that he humble and submit himself before the Supreme Essence. And so we find with Hillel the Elder: when he rejoiced in the rejoicing of the House of the Water-Drawing, he would dance and say: 'If I am here, all are here; if I am not here, who is here?' And Rabban Gamliel and the great Sages would dance there in the Holy Temple, conducting themselves with levity for the sake of the honor of Heaven." "<b>And you already know</b> that the holiest among Israel were the Levites, and Scripture assigned them to walk after the wagons — which is a lowly assignment in human estimation — as it is written (Numbers 7:6–7): ״ויקח משה את העגלות ואת הבקר ויתן אותם אל הלוים. את שתי העגלות ואת ארבעת הבקר נתן לבני גרשון כפי עבודתם, ואת ארבע העגלות ואת שמנת הבקר נתן לבני מררי״. You thus learn that a person is obligated to diminish his own honor for the honor of Heaven. And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'Whoever diminishes his own honor and increases the honor of Heaven — the honor of Heaven increases and his own honor increases. And whoever increases his own honor and diminishes the honor of Heaven — the honor of Heaven remains in its place while his own honor diminishes.'<br><br>And on this matter we find that Yissachar Ish Kfar Barkai was punished, for he would profane the consecrated things of Heaven and honor himself; thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in Pesaḥim at the end of the chapter מקום שנהגו. Four cries went forth in the Temple court:<br><b>The first:</b> 'Depart from here, sons of Eli, for you defiled the Sanctuary of YHWH!'<br><b>Again a cry went forth:</b> 'Depart from here, Yissachar Ish Kfar Barkai, who honored himself and profaned the consecrated things of Heaven!' What would he do? He would wrap his hands in silk and perform the avodah.<br><b>Again a cry went forth:</b> 'Lift up your heads, O gates, and let Yoḥanan ben Narbai, the disciple of Pinkai, enter and fill his belly with the consecrated things of Heaven!' They said of Yoḥanan ben Narbai that he would eat three hundred calves and drink three hundred log-measures of wine, and eat forty se'ah of fledglings at the conclusion of the meal. All the days of Yoḥanan ben Narbai, no leftover (notar) was ever found in the Temple.<br><b>Again a cry went forth:</b> 'Bring me Yishmael ben Fiabi, the disciple of Pinhas, the radiance of the priesthood, and let him serve in the High Priesthood upon the altar!'<br><br>And immediately there, the punishment of Yissachar is mentioned. As they say there: The king and queen were sitting. The king said, 'A young goat is fitting'; the queen said, 'A lamb is fitting.' They said: Who can decide? The High Priest, who ascends with the offerings all day long. They brought Yissachar; he showed with his hand and said: 'If a young goat is fitting, let it be offered for the daily offering (tamid).' The king said: 'Since he showed no fear of the royal authority, cut off his right hand.' Yissachar gave a bribe, and they cut off his left hand. The queen heard and said: 'Let them go back and cut off his right hand as well.' Said Rav Yosef: Blessed be the Merciful One, who took Yissachar and made him a laughingstock in this world — that is, it was better for him to receive his punishment in this world than to be punished in the World to Come.<br><br>And so we find with Shebna, who was over the household, who was a priest according to the view of some of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, based on what is written (Isaiah 22:21): ״והלבשתיו כתנתך״; and because he would demean the offerings, he was punished, and Sennacherib king of Assyria killed him. And concerning him it is said (ibid.): ״תמוש היתד התקועה במקום נאמן״, and a prophecy was uttered concerning his punishment.<br><br><b>The third matter:</b> The great miracle performed with the altar. This altar is the altar of burnt-offering; it was made of shittim wood and overlaid with copper the thickness of a gold dinar, and the fire upon the altar burned continually and was never extinguished — yet the copper was not melted and the wood was not consumed. This was one of the eighteen miracles that occurred in the Temple. And on account of this he repeated a second time: ״ואש המזבח תוקד בו לא תכבה״ — for it had already been stated ״ואש המזבח תוקד בו״. Rather, it comes to allude to this miracle, as if to say: behold a wondrous thing done with the altar — the fire upon the altar burns in it, it is not extinguished, and the altar endures.<br><br><b>The fourth matter:</b> The greatness of the punishment of the wicked in the torments of Gehinnom. And because of this he mentioned a third time: ״אש תמיד תוקד על המזבח לא תכבה״. For the altar is the place of atonement, but the wicked did not seek atonement; Scripture alludes to you that they are sentenced in the fire of Gehinnom — an everlasting fire, since they denied the everlasting fire. This is the very matter with which Isaiah the prophet, peace be upon him, would warn and rebuke the wicked, as he said (Isaiah 33:14): ״פחדו בציון חטאים אחזה רעדה חנפים מי יגור לנו אש אוכלה מי יגור לנו מוקדי עולם״. He would frighten them about that everlasting fire in Zion which they denied, and did not repent, and did not seek atonement. And this is how Scripture concludes: ״מי יגור לנו אש אוכלה מי יגור לנו מוקדי עולם״ — he comes to instill fear about the fire of Gehinnom, concerning which it is written (ibid. 66): ״כי תולעתם לא תמות ואשם לא תכבה״, in the same language as is stated here: תמיד לא תכבה." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> <b>It is the burnt-offering.</b> This is Rome, which exalts itself and raises itself up. Further there: anyone who exalts himself and raises himself up — his end is to be judged by fire. For the generation of the Flood, because they grew proud and said (Job 21:15): ״מה שדי כי נעבדנו״, were judged by fire, as it is said (ibid. v. 6): ״בעת יזורבו נצמתו בחומו נדעכו ממקומם״. The people of Sodom, because they grew proud — as it is said (Ezekiel 16:49): ״הנה זה היה עון סדום אחותך גאון שבעת לחם ושלות השקט היה לה ולבנותיה״ — were judged by fire, as it is said (Genesis 19:24): ״וה׳ המטיר על סדום ועל עמורה גפרית ואש״. And so Pharaoh, because he grew proud and said (Exodus 5:2): ״מי ה׳ אשר אשמע בקולו״, and said (Ezekiel 29:3): ״לי יאורי ואני עשיתני״, was judged by fire, as it is said (Psalms 18:14): ״וירעם בשמים ה׳ ועליון יתן קולו ברד וגחלי אש״, and it is written (Exodus 9:24): ״ויהי ברד ואש מתלקחת״. And so Sennacherib, because he grew proud and said (2 Kings 19:23): ״אני עליתי מרום הרים ירכתי לבנון״ and so forth, was judged by fire, as it is said (Isaiah 10:16): ״ותחת כבודו יקד יקוד כיקוד אש״. And so Nebuchadnezzar, because he grew proud and exalted himself and said (ibid. 14): ״אעלה על במתי עב אדמה לעליון״, and said (ibid.): ״ממעל לכוכבי אל ארים כסאי״ — therefore his armies were judged by fire. You find that when they entered the dedication of the image, Scripture enumerated eight nations, as it is said (Daniel 3:3): ״באדין מתכנשין אחשדרפניא סגניא ופחותא אדרגזריא גדבריא דתבריא תפתיא וכל שלטוני מדינתא״ — here are eight. And in the second account there are only four nations, as it is said (ibid.): ״ומתכנשין אחשדרפניא סגניא ופחותא והדברי מלכא״, and those four nations slew a multitude by the sparks of fire. And so in the future, because she exalts herself and raises herself up, as it is written (Obadiah 1:4): ״אם תגביה כנשר ואם בין כוכבים שים קנך״, therefore they will be judged by fire, as it is said (Daniel 7:11): ״חזה הוית עד די קטילת חיותא והובד גשמה ויהיבת ליקידת אשא״. End of the Midrash." "<b>And as for</b> the fact that Scripture mentions 'Command Aaron and his sons' — Scripture put Aaron before his sons, because throughout the entire section of Vayikra it is written וזרקו בני אהרן, and וארכו בני אהרן, and Aaron was not mentioned there because he was distanced on account of the incident of the calf. Moses said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of the universe, 'a hated well, yet its waters are precious.' If for the wood You apportioned honor for the sake of their children — as we learned: all woods are fit for the arrangement except olive wood and vine wood — how much more so for Aaron for the sake of his children. Therefore it says 'Command Aaron and his sons' — He put Aaron before his sons." "<b>By way of reason (sekhel):</b> 'This is the law of the burnt offering — it is the burnt offering' — Scripture alludes to the excellence of the rational soul, as if to say: this is the law of the soul; the burnt offering — it is the burnt offering, by the merit of the Torah, which is called 'she,' as it is said (Proverbs 3:18): ״עץ חיים היא למחזיקים בה״, and it is written (Psalms 19:8): ״תורת ה׳ תמימה משיבת נפש״, that is, the soul to its root. <b>It is the burnt offering, upon the fires, upon the altar.</b> This comes to explain the excellence of the rational soul, that it ascends above the excellence of the spheres and above the excellence of the angels, for the word על מוקדה embraces the spheres and the word על המזבח embraces all the angels. And the meaning of על מוקדה is as though it said 'upon the fire,' as in the verse (Isaiah 10:16): ״יקד יקוד כיקוד אש״, and he means to say: upon the fire, upon the sun, for the sphere of the sun moves the element of fire just as the sphere of the moon moves the element of water. And behold, King Solomon, peace be upon him, all whose words in the book of Ecclesiastes were 'under the sun,' mentioned regarding the soul (Ecclesiastes 3:16): 'it ascends above' — that is, above the sun, which is 'upon its fire.'" "<b>And know that</b> the sphere of the sun embraces all the spheres, being in the middle, and in all things the essence lies in its middleness, and he comes to allude that the soul is above the excellence of the spheres. And in his saying 'upon the altar,' it is an allusion to the excellence of the angels, and the word מזבח is a comprehensive name for them, because just as the altar is a designated place for the sacrifices, which are the will of HaShem, may He be exalted — as it is written 'a pleasing fragrance,' and its Aramaic translation is לאתקבלא ברעוא — and Scripture says (Isaiah 56:7): ״לרצון על מזבחי״, so the angels draw near to Him to do His will, as 'mighty in strength to heed the voice of His word.' And in his saying 'all the night until the morning,' he comes to explain by what merit it ascends and by what power it is above the excellence of the spheres and the angels: by the merit that it engages in Torah all the night until the morning. For it ascends and is praised all the days of its being in the body, which is darkness and gloom, 'until the morning' — that is, until it separates from it and returns to her father's house as in her youth, which is her true morning, a morning without clouds; as he says, 'and the fire of the altar shall burn in it' — the intention is to say that although the fire of the angels burns, and their fire is great and mighty, she, the soul, ascends above them all; and this is along the lines of the statement of the Rabbis, of blessed memory: 'Greater are the righteous than the ministering angels.'" "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> <b>it is the burnt offering</b>, like והיא מתהלכת, and it is the attribute that ascends to the Place from which it was hewn; from it begins the thought of the one who offers, and I have already mentioned this matter in Parashat Vayikra."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {3 "The Hebrew switches mid-comment from רז״ל to a block of four themes (הענין השלישי, הרביעי); the <br> tags and bolded headers are preserved from the source. The lengthy Aramaic passages from Pesaḥim are translated as they stand, retaining the Talmudic narrative voice.", 4 "The Aramaic phrase די נורא at the end of the Daniel citation is rendered literally: 'slew a multitude by the sparks of fire.' The closing ע\"כ במדרש ('end of the Midrash') is rendered as 'End of the Midrash.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:20.319050215Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}